    "depth_and_pile": {
      "group": "plan-logic",
      "binding": "specified",
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "single-pile",
          "status": "canonical"
        },
        {
          "id": "double-pile",
          "status": "forbidden",
          "note": "Contradicts the family's whole logic: every member's plan is generated by span, light and airflow limits that a double-pile depth defeats."
        }
      ],
      "rule": "Every member of the family is fundamentally single-pile -- one room deep, or in the log and shotgun cases one room WIDE with depth built by repetition -- because the plan is generated by physical limits (log-handling length, cross-ventilation, daylight reach) rather than by a proportional canon that could absorb a second pile.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "status": "drafted",
      "note": "Directly and independently documented across all four direct style children: american-farmhouse-vernacular's own defining_characteristics ('single-pile or shallow room depth carried well into the twentieth century so rooms light and ventilate from two sides'); log-vernacular-american's pen logic (16-20 ft wide, one room, growth by ADDING pens rather than deepening one); shotgun-house ('one room wide... no hallway... rooms open directly into one another'); charleston-single-house's own hard constraint (c02, 'the plan must remain single-pile: one room on each side of the stair passage'). Both log variants restate it directly too: appalachian-log-house and dogtrot-vernacular both list 'single-pile section throughout so that every room ventilates across' as a defining characteristic."
    },
    "daylight_strategy": {
      "group": "plan-logic",
      "binding": "specified",
      "rule": "The single-pile section is the whole daylighting and ventilation strategy: every principal room is meant to have operable openings on two opposite (or nearly opposite) walls so that light and cross-breeze reach every room without mechanical assistance. Openings themselves are minimal and structurally constrained in the earliest log work, but the underlying strategic move -- section before ornament -- is the same.",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "judgment": true,
      "status": "drafted",
      "note": "charleston-single-house states it as a hard requirement (c02: 'operable windows on two opposite walls of every principal room'); american-farmhouse-vernacular's defining_characteristics state it directly; shotgun-house's own hard constraint (c03) requires 'operable windows on both side walls of every room' as the section's entire cooling mechanism; dogtrot-vernacular's defining_characteristics: 'single-pile section throughout so that every room ventilates across.' log-vernacular-american's own text is the partial case: its pens are single-room and therefore trivially satisfy the two-wall principle, but its windows are small and few by structural necessity ('windows small, often under 3 square feet in early work') rather than by daylighting ambition -- the strategy is shared, its full expression is not, which is why this is marked judgment rather than measured."
    },
